</div>&nbsp;=20
<p></p>
<h1 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-1Statementofpurpose">1 Statement of purpose=
</h1>
<p>The purpose of this document is to provide a set of industry-proven stan=
dards for naming conceptual, logical, and physical data objects. By agreein=
g, as an organisation, to follow a common standard in the naming of data ob=
jects, lays the foundation in establish a solid understanding of the flow o=
f information within and between application systems. This understanding pr=
omotes reuse and flexibility as systems develop.</p>
<h2 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-1.1Applicabilityandscope">1.1 Applicability=
 and scope</h2>
<p>This document provides naming standards for the following objects: <br c=
lass=3D"atl-forced-newline">
  Conceptual/Logical Data Model</p>
<ul>
 <li>Data Model (CDM, LDM)</li>
 <li>Subject Area</li>
 <li>Entity</li>
 <li>Logical View</li>
 <li>Attribute</li>
 <li>Relationship</li>
 <li>Data Domain</li>
</ul>
<p><br class=3D"atl-forced-newline">
  Physical Data Model (not DBMS specific)</p>
<ul>
 <li>Table</li>
 <li>Physical View</li>
 <li>Column</li>
 <li>Primary Key Constraint</li>
 <li>Primary Index</li>
 <li>Foreign Key Constraint</li>
 <li>Join Index</li>
 <li>Unique Constraint</li>
 <li>Unique Index</li>
 <li>Non-Unique Index</li>
 <li>Check Constraint</li>
</ul>
<p>The standards and guidelines set out in this document should be applied =
to new developments only. It is not intended that existing systems be chang=
ed to adhere to the standards outlined.</p>
<h1 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2ConceptualandLogicalDataModelNamingStandar=
ds">2 Conceptual and Logical Data Model Naming Standards</h1>
<h2 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2.1Entity&amp;AttributeNamingConventionsand=
Standards">2.1 Entity &amp; Attribute Naming Conventions and Standards</h2>
<p>Conceptual Data Modelling should be based on Data Subject Area definitio=
ns containing Entity Relationship diagrams which describe the business rule=
s surrounding core information management concepts of the business. These D=
ata Subject Area Data Models need to be version controlled across the vario=
us life cycle stages which include Solution Outline, Macro Design, Micro De=
sign/Build Cycle/Deployment &amp; Maintenance and Archive. <br class=3D"atl=
-forced-newline">
  Logical Data Modelling should follow the normalization process using Enti=
ty Relationship models for OLTP based applications and denormalized Star Sc=
hema/Snowflake models for Dimensional/Data Mart based applications. For dat=
a warehouse staging layers the Data Vault (hubs/links/satellites) model sho=
uld be used.</p>
<h2 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2,2Entity&amp;AttributeMetaDataStandards">2=
,2 Entity &amp; Attribute Meta Data Standards</h2>
<p>The LV Data Directory is the source of the Business Glossary, Data Dicti=
onary and Data Quality processes. The Data Directory should be reviewed as =
part of the data modelling design process so that standard business terms /=
 attribute names can be re-used wherever possible.<br>
  The Data Directory supports general properties for managing entity and at=
tribute definitions. These properties define general meta data required to =
manage entity and attribute definitions from an enterprise perspective. As =
part of the data modelling design process, any new business terms / data it=
ems will need to be added to the Data Directory. <br class=3D"atl-forced-ne=
wline">
  Refer to the Data Directory guidelines for information on how to provide =
input into this process.</p>
<h2 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2.3DataNamingamongstCommonRuleSets">2.3 Dat=
a Naming amongst Common Rule Sets</h2>
<p>*<em>Rule Set Hierarchy</em>*Many of the naming rules are shared among t=
he data objects. This section groups these rules in rule sets. The diagram =
below illustrates how these rules are shared. <br class=3D"atl-forced-newli=
ne"><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-man=
ual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" heig=
ht=3D"304" width=3D"468" src=3D"d27f31f7313a76fd190bb214d8ac10400554221855c=
55b8fedf28b88fac9af8b" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/18228179/wor=
ddava6fd6cc7432a6d953bedd659255f0430.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=
=3D1457627507000&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-lin=
ked-resource-id=3D"18228181" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked=
-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"worddav=
a6fd6cc7432a6d953bedd659255f0430.png" data-base-url=3D"https://confluence.g=
roup.net" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resou=
rce-container-id=3D"18228179" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"116=
" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D""></span></p>
<h3 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2.3.1CommonRuleSet=E2=80=93AllLogicalandPhy=
sicalObjectNames">2.3.1 Common Rule Set =E2=80=93 All Logical and Physical =
Object Names</h3>
<p><br class=3D"atl-forced-newline">
  Overall naming principles<br><strong><em>Clarity</em></strong> - the obje=
ct name is clear and unambiguous in appearance and meaning.<br><strong><em>=
Simplicity</em></strong> - the object name is expressed in simple or uncomb=
ined terms, avoiding needless complexity.<br><strong><em>'Shareability'</em=
></strong> - the object name is usable throughout the enterprise.<br><stron=
g><em>Recognisability</em></strong> - the object name can be recognised/ide=
ntified from past experience or knowledge.<br><strong><em>Sensibility</em><=
/strong> - the object name will create a positive or favourable impression.=
<br><strong><em>Consistency</em></strong> - the object name is reliable (wi=
th uniformity of the name when compared to other names).<br><strong><em>Con=
formance to rules of syntax</em></strong> =E2=80=93 the object name follows=
 the proper format/syntax described in the corresponding naming guidelines.=
</p>
<ul>
 <li>Be clear on what word types should/shouldn't be used.</li>
 <li>Don't use adverbs (words like <em>never, always</em>, and all those <e=
m>-ly</em> words.).</li>
 <li>Don't use articles (<em>the, a, an</em>, etc.).</li>
 <li>Don't use pronouns (he, she, it, them, these, those, etc.).</li>
 <li>Don't use negative words in a name (<em>not</em>, <em>non</em>). Posit=
ive names are always better.</li>
 <li>Avoid the use of prepositions (<em>to, in, on, from,</em> etc.) unless=
 there is no other way. They can usually be avoided by changing the word or=
der. For example, <em>Birth Date</em> rather than <em>Date of Birth</em>.</=
li>
 <li>Don't use conjunctions such as <em>and, nor, but, or</em>. Their prese=
nce usually means that you're trying to give a name to something that repre=
sents more than one thing.</li>
 <li>Avoid comparatives and superlatives. Adjectives with <em>-er</em> and =
<em>-est</em> can usually be replaced by the base form of the word as with =
<em>low</em> rather than <em>lowest</em> in a name such as <em>Low Credit L=
imit Amt</em>.</li>
 <li>Don't include vague or indefinite words: <em>any, other, some,</em> an=
d the like.</li>
 <li>Avoid jargon and insider terminology. Remember the <em>stranger test</=
em>: if a stranger to the business can read a name and have a good idea wha=
t it might be about, it's usually a good name.</li>
 <li>Watch out for synonyms. English has many ways to say the same thing. T=
ry to use the simplest variant and be consistent across all names. For inst=
ance <em>start</em> can be used instead of <em>begin</em> or <em>beginning<=
/em> in all names.</li>
 <li>Watch for variant spellings. If a word can be spelled more than one wa=
y, use the simplest. E.g., <em>judgment</em> and not <em>judgement</em>.</l=
i>
 <li>Don't use possessives: Customer Last Name not Customer's Last Name.</l=
i>
 <li>Avoid past tense and other endings on words unless you really need the=
m for clarity. For example, <em>Credit Limit Change Date</em> is as clear a=
s <em>Credit Limit Changed Date</em>. <em>Market Cost Amt</em> and <em>Mark=
eting Cost Amt</em>, though, are almost surely different.</li>
 <li>Acronyms must be used in upper case, regardless of the case rule for t=
he data object being named.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2.3.2LogicalAttributeandPhysicalColumnNames=
">2.3.2 Logical Attribute and Physical Column Names</h3>
<p>The naming of attribute and column names should be constructed with a Pr=
ime Word, an optional Qualifier(s) and a Class Word.&nbsp;</p>
<h4 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2.3.2.1PrimeWord(required)">2.3.2.1 Prime W=
ord (required)</h4>
<p>A prime word represents the business portion of the attribute and identi=
fies the major category or subject area in which the attribute belongs. <br=
 class=3D"atl-forced-newline">
  Examples:</p>
<ul>
 <li>Employee</li>
 <li>Customer</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2.3.2.2Qualifier(s)(optional)">2.3.2.2 Qual=
ifier(s) (optional)&nbsp;</h4>
<p>A qualifier is used to further describe a Prime Word and make it unique =
within a context. <br class=3D"atl-forced-newline">
  Examples:</p>
<ul>
 <li>An entity ORDER may contain one or more data attributes such as <em>Or=
der Entry Date</em> or <em>Order Completion Date</em> where <em>Entry</em> =
and <em>Completion</em> are the qualifiers.</li>
 <li>In the data attribute <em>Cost Budget Period Amt</em>, the components =
<em>Budget</em> and <em>Period</em> are qualifiers. Try to keep the number =
of qualifiers to a minimum.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"LVDataNamingStandards-2.3.2.3ClassWord(required)">2.3.2.3 Class W=
ord (required)</h4>
<p>A class word identifies the type of data or major classification to whic=
h an attribute belongs. e.g. Code or Rate. <br class=3D"atl-forced-newline"=
>
  Class words shall be taken from the controlled list below. Where possible=
, the Logical/Physical classword should be used. In circumstances where the=
 DBMS column length has limitations, then the Physical Abbreviation can be =
used. The class word used should be consistent throughout the data model, t=
here should not be a mixture of class words and abbreviated class words:-</=
p>
